# Children of the Five Winds — New Character Options

**Version:** 0.4.1
**Source:** Legend of the Five Rings: Children of the Five Winds
**Publisher:** Fantasy Flight Games

---

## New Advantages and Disadvantages

This section introduces new distinctions, passions, adversities, and anxieties that complement those that can be found in Chapter 2 of the Legend of the Five Rings roleplaying game core rulebook.

### SPECIFIC DISTINCTIONS

These distinctions follow the same format as those in Chapter 2 of the core rulebook.

#### Affinity with [Animal Type] (Void)

In the hollow of a tree, the little girl had left some cooked rice for the crows. Sometimes she would leave a bit of fish or seaweed too. She had even taught her dog, Yuki, to leave the tree alone. Every day, the birds would leave behind something small in exchange. A shiny stone. A bit of metal. A colorful piece of paper. This time when the girl approached the tree, she saw a whole murder of crows, eerily silent. They had left a ring, one lost by the village elder years ago.

Types: Fame, Interpersonal Effects: The following apply to a character with the Affinity with [Animal Type] distinction:

#### Legal Scholarship (Air)

Katatsune gleefully opened his newly bought Qamarist scroll. His fingers brushed over the bold, artistic calligraphy; the reflective golden edges; and the confident, red-and-black illustrations. "Look how the text mirrors the falling of leaves. And here! The illustration of a frog. So whimsical!" Nomolun chuckled. "This is what you came to Khanbulak for? Pretty pictures?"

"No, this is an expansive legal document on educational law—" He sheepishly looked up from the scroll. "And for the pretty pictures, yes."

Types: Interpersonal, Social Effects: The following apply to a character with the Legal Scholarship distinction:

#### Syncretic Philosophy (Water)

Mirumoto Umeyo stood between the magistrate and the village elder, tempers heated as they debated the fate of the thief, currently in chains. "Justice is quite clear," the young magistrate said. "This thief has stolen many times and has been unrepentant, so he must give up his life. Only then will the offenses ever end." "The crimes are minor. And his family will suffer with his death," the village elder said, her back stooped and leaning on the magistrate's arm. Umeyo couldn't help but smile at the magistrate's discomfort, caught between an official's sense of propriety and mercy for the elder. She decided to lean on this mercy when she said, "Of course the thief must give up his life, but not in that way." The other two women looked at her, puzzled. "Other lands interpret life in different ways. Give the thief's 'life' to me. I will mold him into a different person. And thus his old 'life' will end, but a new one shall begin."

Types: Interpersonal, Mental Effects: The following apply to a character with the Syncretic Philosophy distinction:

### SPECIFIC PASSIONS

These passions follow the same format as those in Chapter 2 of the core rulebook.

#### Horse Racing (Air)

Ichi's parents gasped as she leapt from the boulder to land on the back of an unbridled horse. One moment she stood between them. The next moment she had crossed the field to "talk" with the horse. The beast shot across the field, galloping in great, thunderous strides. Her parents ran after her, knowing they would never be able to catch them. That horse had been bred and trained for speed. Ichi's screams echoed across the air, and for a moment, their hearts leapt to their throats, until they realized the screams were of joy.

Types: Physical Effects: The following apply to a character with the Horse Racing passion:

#### Hot Pot (Earth)

Möngke sat on his horse—back aching, stomach growling—as the night grew deeper. It had been a long, dusty journey. His stomach growled again, but this time in anticipation: of the fiery bite of chili; the heady, back-of-the-throat warmth of herbs; and the satisfying aroma of meaty fats. Urging his horse faster and faster, he finally came across the encampment, flew into his family's yurt, and slid in front of the hot pot, bowl already in hand. "Möngke travels on his stomach!" his aunts and uncles laughed. "His nose will always bring him back home."

Types: Interpersonal, Physical Effects: The following apply to a character with the Hot Pot passion:

#### Stargazing (Void)

Rashid held the astrolabe aloft and lined it up with the North Star. "Children, with this you can determine direction and—" He spun around and saw no children in sight. He sighed. I could be unlocking the mysteries of the heavens right now, revealing empyrean wonders, he thought. Suppressed childish giggles clued Rashid in to their presence in the garden. He sighed again, wandered over, and saw them all lying on their backs, hands outstretched, reaching for the stars. He chuckled and lay next to the children, listening to them rename the stars to their design.

## Specific Adversities

These adversities follow the same format as those in Chapter 2 of the core rulebook.

### Lost Family History (Earth)

Iuchi Ryomaru cleaned the headstones with water and bitterly pulled at the weeds. This family graveyard had been left uncared for, a sign of his failures. With the pulling of each weed, he wished the Fortunes would help him find the missing poems, journals, and histories of his great-great-grandparents' generation. He had journeyed to Otosan Uchi, the Miya Library, the Izaku Library, and Khanbulak searching for copies of their lost texts. He had dedicated his whole life to the search, unwittingly neglecting his duties at the graveyard.

Types: Interpersonal, Material Effects: The following apply to a character with the Lost Family History adversity:

### Penalty (Fire)

Asahina Sora knelt on the padded cushion, holding aloft this year's scroll. Moto Chizue unrolled the scroll to examine Sora's calligraphy. Chizue did not actually care about the calligraphy itself, but Sora's brushwork had improved. She also did not care about the contents of the scroll; it had the usual flowery apology and praise of Chizue's eloquence. She only cared about the process itself, a yearly reminder of Sora humiliating her at Otosan Uchi, and how they had to continue this apology for the next ten years.

Types: Infamy, Social Effects: The following apply to a character with the Penalty adversity:

### Schism in Status (Water)

"Isawa Yasu may be respected among shugenja as an ishiken," the courtiers at Otosan Uchi say, "but to give their heart to a forbidden love in such a blatant way… incomprehensible." Perhaps Yasu's dedication to wearing that favored comb in their hair all the time may raise some eyebrows, but the harsh gossip outweighs any real offense. They refuse to allow the rumors to weaken their heart; Yasu finds strength in their lingering love, even if it makes their life in court difficult.

Types: Infamy, Interpersonal Effects: The following apply to a character with the Schism in Status adversity:

## Specific Anxieties

These anxieties follow the same format as those in Chapter 2 of the core rulebook.

### Animal Signs (Void)

When the two rōnin saw the black cat, Junko ran after it while Eijiro remained still and stiff as a board. "Stop chasing the beast!" Eijiro shouted. "But black cats are lucky," Junko's voice said from behind a building. "Yes, but causing it to run away chases the good luck away!" "Really?" Junko's voice said, somewhere behind some wagons. "I've never heard of that." "Yes!" he hissed. "Now get back here!" "Aww." Junko sighed. "You just don't like cats." "Both can be true at the same time!" He jumped slightly at a small, black shadow streaking across his path.

Types: Mental, Spiritual Effects: The following apply to a character with the Animal Signs anxiety:

### Disdain for Urban Sprawl (Earth)

Agasha Etsu held her sleeve against her face. "I prefer the clean mountain air." "The thick scent of corruption disturbed Etsu-san's senses," Kitsuki Moriko said, lowering her voice in a mocking tone. "She surveyed the scene, seeing evil reflected in the eyes of the people before her." "Please stop narrating me into your investigator stories." The older woman sighed. "As the darkness swallowed the City of the Rich Frog, Etsu-san hardened her gaze, determined to pierce the shadows to illuminate the truth."

"We're standing in broad daylight!" Etsu took a deep breath, trying to summon patience from the ground beneath her. Moriko, in her own way, was only pointing out Etsu's folly. If they were to solve this mystery, she had to overcome her annoyance at this busy, chaotic city.

#### Tip of the Tongue (Air)

Orghina sucked her teeth in frustration. She knew the Rokugani word. Except the Rokugani word refused to leap from her mind and down her tongue, and the Rokugani merchant smiling politely in front of her would not have any idea what the Ujik equivalent meant. "Are you looking for dried fish?" the merchant said. Orghina shook her head. "No, it's not food." "A netsuke?" "No, you don't wear it!" "Ah! Of course, I have a set of tantō you might like." "No! You don't…nothing that stabs." The merchant then went on a litany of Rokugani nouns as Orghina sat down on a stool, head in her hands.

## New Samurai Heritages

A variety of unique heritages arise from having a family lineage derived in part from the Unicorn's history. Even non-Unicorn characters may take on one of these heritages because their family has a connection to a place outside Rokugan or to the wilderness. Because of the Unicorn's unique connection to lands outside Rokugan, they have access to more syncretic or diverse heritages, whether for good or for ill.

Table 2-1: New Samurai Heritages presents backgrounds for characters connected to the Unicorn or to lands outside Rokugan. This table can be used instead of Table 2-1: Samurai Heritage on page 96 of the core rulebook and should be employed in the same manner.

## New Gear and Items

The following are new items, armor, and other equipment that can be used in your games. Many are associated with the strong trade relations of the Unicorn Clan and the peoples of foreign lands, and any inclusion in a game should be approved by the Game Master.

### NEW WEAPONS

This sections includes a collection of new weapons, some unique and others similar to those available in the core rulebook, with minor changes to represent the cultural and manufacturing differences between Rokugan and other lands.

#### Catalpa Bow

Shrine folklore holds that the mere pluck of a bowstring can banish ghosts, and that yumi made from the tonewood of the catalpa tree are especially attractive to elemental spirits and repellent to malicious ones. The Kitsune Forest is rich with soft catalpa wood, which the Fox Clan uses for bow making (among other things). The Fox regularly trade catalpa yumi to the Phoenix Clan's Kaito family, for whom the wood is relatively rare. However, the Fox are careful to utilize catalpa only from trees that have fallen naturally; they believe that hewing catalpa will "frighten" the wood, reversing the effect of the sacred tone.

#### Charging Lance

While relatively uncommon in Rokugan, lances are sometimes used where flat lands make unified cavalry charges a viable option for battle. The lance is a specialized spear designed exclusively for use on horseback, either to break the enemy in an initial charge or to hurl from horseback like a javelin.

While most warriors favor the flexibility of the spear over the lance, Moto tactics make extensive use of cavalry lancers, usually to capitalize on openings created by archers. Enemies who break formation and spread to avoid arrows simply play into the Moto's hands, as they are now perfect fodder for the lancers' backbreaking charge.

#### Fire Lance

The use of black powder is strictly regulated in the Empire of Rokugan. While fireworks and certain demolitions are permitted, Imperial decree forbids this powder's use in weaponry or sieges. The Ganzu people are not bound by such decrees, deploying the fire lance as a means to defend the Sand Road from afar and as a cavalry countermeasure. In its most rudimentary form, the fire lance is a straight-flying firework attached to a polearm, but more advanced versions—like those with metal barrels or that fire pellets—are becoming more common.

The fire lance is a polearm when used as such. While its wielder is aiming the rocket, it counts as a ranged weapon.

#### Ganzu Ring Ax

Among the most unusual battle-axes brandished by warriors of the Hidden Valley's Ganzu people, the ring ax is a large, bladed disk affixed to a two-handed shaft. Associated with guardians of the Sand Road, the ring ax has a disk-shaped blade. It is wielded in a martial style that utilizes the momentum of each strike so as to conserve energy. Unicorn Clan samurai sometimes call this weapon a coin ax due to the blade's resemblance to a Rokugani coin.

#### Katar (Punch Dagger)

Widely adopted throughout the nations surrounding the Sand Road and standard armament among the Ganzu people, the katar is a large, triangular dagger that is wielded in an H-shaped horizontal grip, with a guard protecting the knuckles. Often worn in pairs (khatari), this weapon has a wedge-shaped blade designed not only for slashing but also specifically for splitting and punching through shields and hardened armor.

### Table 2-2: New Melee Weapons

### Table 2-3: New Ranged Weapons

### Table 2-4: New Specialist Weapons

#### Repeating Crossbow

While the repeating crossbow is utilized along the length of the Sand Road, it is rarely seriously considered for battlefield deployment. Instead, it is typically used for home defense, at sea, and by the guards of al-Zawira. It has weaker projectiles and a shorter range than the standard crossbow, but it makes up for this with a wooden quarrel stock that reloads the crossbow after every draw, provided there is ammunition available (maximum 4 bolts). It is also small enough to be spanned by hand instead of by foot or special mechanism. It is said that this device inspired the invention of the Kaiu no oyumi (rapid-fire crossbow) in the last century.

#### Saddle Cutter

Among the many lessons the Ujik taught the Ki-Rin during their initial raids on Shinjo's clan was to remove any advantage the enemy believed they had. The Ujik saddle cutter is one such combat equalizer. This flexible, sharp, and serrated blade is designed to cut an opponent's stirrups while the wielder rides alongside them—or in skilled hands, to snip a saddle's straps, severing rider from steed. It is said that Utaku and Shinjo both hated this weapon and denounced it as underhanded and disrespectful, until Ide pointed out that when properly used, the weapon benevolently spares both horse and rider the bite of steel. Even so, few outside the Moto family or Ujik tribes practice its use today.

When you make an attack with a saddle cutter, you may spend ⚙ as follows: ⚙+: This attack deals no damage to the target; instead, it severs the target's stirrups, saddle, or other connection to their mount. The target must succeed on a TN 2 Fitness (Earth or Water) check to remain mounted. The TN of this check increases by a number equal to the number of ⚙ spent.

### New Shields

This section includes two new shield options new shield options players can choose from when playing schools who specialize in their use.

#### Ide Parasol Shield

An innovation of Ide family Imperial peacekeepers (and serving as their informal badge of office), the parasol shield is a folding rattan-cloth parasol with thin tin or brass plates hidden in the canopy, which is disguised beneath a layer of patterned silk. Considerably heavier than a typical parasol, a parasol shield is usually opened and stuck into the ground at an angle to provide cover, but it can also be used to protect against arrows. This parasol was invented to protect against bandit ambushes targeting diplomatic caravans.

While this parasol shield's canopy is fully extended and readied, you treat your physical resistance as 3.

As a Movement and Support action, while the canopy is readied, you may increase the TN of the next Attack check targeting you by 1. This effect persists until the end of your next turn.

#### Standing Shield

The standing shield is a heavy rectangular braced shield that is typically carried by a designated shield bearer, providing near-complete protection for both the bearer and the warrior behind them. Ashigaru bearing a standing shield are not permitted to retreat,

as this would create gaps in the defense and expose their allies. These shields can also be propped to stand on their own, providing temporary cover on the battlefield. While you have a readied standing shield, or while it is deployed standing on its own in front of you, you treat your physical resistance as 6. As a Movement and Support action, while you or a nearby bearer have the standing shield readied or deployed, you may increase the TN of Attack action checks made targeting you by 2. This effect persists until the start of your next turn, as long as you are behind the standing shield.

The items included in this book are those that might be found along the Sand Road or are commonly used or seen among the Unicorn Clan. Each item's rarity assumes it is being sought within Rokugan. In other circumstances, the GM should adjust the rarity appropriately.

#### Astrolabe

Among the most useful of pocket mathematical tools, astrolabes come in many forms, from a metal disk with shapes carved into its face; to a fist-sized globe with rotating rings interlocked along its body; and even to the mariner's astrolabe, which is held in both hands. The many functions of this tool include timekeeping, distance triangulation and estimation, mathematical calculation, and tracking the celestial bodies of the night sky. (Cost: 1 koku. Rarity: 8.)

#### Horo (Arrow Cloak)

One of the oldest antiarrow innovations in Rokugan is the inflating arrow cloak, which is essentially a flexible, wicker-framed sail made of high-thread-count silk. Attached to a horseback rider at the waist and shoulders, the arrow cloak fills with air as the horse gallops, puffing out into a balloon shape. Arrows either bounce off the billowing cloak or are caught by the cloth before they can reach the rider. The arrow cloak has become a symbol of battlefield messengers and Imperial Heralds alike, but while it grants the rider significant protection from ranged attacks, it also makes them a target, as there is significant prestige in being the archer who killed an arrow cloak wearer. While wearing a deployed horo, treat your physical resistance against arrows and similar missiles as 5. While riding on horseback or otherwise mounted, increase this resistance to 6. (Cost: 5 koku. Rarity: 6.)

#### Lantern (Personal)

Lanterns of all shapes and sorts are common throughout the Empire, and the craft of lantern making is considered one of Rokugan's greatest arts. The most typical personal lantern consists of a bamboo frame with paper stretched over the ribs to protect the candle within from the wind. Lanterns can be mounted on staves, carried by handles, hung from hooks, set to rest on stilts, or even designed to float on water. They can also be shaped like fantastic animals, painted, or formed from patterned paper. Metal or wooden lanterns are common as well, if a bit pricier. (Cost: 1 bu. Rarity: 2.)

#### Mineral-Oil Lamp

Most Rokugani personal lanterns use a candle as their light source, while standing and stone lanterns use rapeseed or sardine oil with a pith wick. Alchemists of the Qamarist Caliphate have used a refined oil made from coal and certain clays since long ago, and while only the purest of such oil burns clean enough to be used indoors, the resulting mineral-oil lamps burn brighter and longer than those that use whale or rapeseed oil. A mere vial of the "glass naphtha" can cost a farmer's yearly wage in Rokugan, and alchemists of several clans are secretly trying to reproduce it. (Cost: 6 koku. Rarity: 8.)

#### Prayer Rug

One of the most important personal effects for those who worship the Nameless Prophet, the prayer rug is a thick carpet that is used for kneeling upon during daily prayers. Such rugs come in a wide variety of patterns, but all incorporate religious symbology of the Qamarist faith, aiding the user in their prayers. (Cost: 2 koku. Rarity: 9.)

#### Sand Clock

Personal timepieces have become status symbols among the Rokugani elite, as traditional water clocks are larger, more common, and less expensive. The most prized timepieces are sand clocks, the best examples of which come from al-Zawira. They consist of two glass-bulb ampoules partially filled with powdered marble or ash, expertly measured so that it takes a certain amount of time for sand to pass from one bulb to the other. The glassblowing techniques required to make the sand clock are not exclusive to the artisans of the Caliphate, but access to better materials, the skill required to make smaller examples, and the artistic

#### Table 2-5: New Armor

elegance typical of the devices have created a cottage industry in al-Zawira for sand clocks calibrated for the Rokugani hour. (Cost: 2 koku. Rarity: 6.)

#### Traveling Board Games

Games of luck and skill are popular throughout the world, and traveling versions are often carried on long trips. The most common are contained within hinged boxes, many of which fold out into a game board and contain the pieces required by the game. The most sophisticated may even use magnets to keep pieces from falling off the board, although these are exceptional examples. Most are simple games like Go; shōgi; sugoroku; or ones from distant lands, like hounds and jackals or senet. For some, playing a game can be a means to relax on a stressful trip, or even akin to personal meditation. (Cost: 1 bu. Rarity: 4.)

#### Word of the Prophet

While the Word of the Prophet is an illuminated tome that is banned within Rokugani borders, countless copies of it circulate along the Sand Road, and some have even made their way within the Emerald Empire's borders. The book, a collection of Qamarist stories, is the basis of worship for the Qamarist faith. It contains not only the story of the Nameless Prophet but also an extensive appendix of notes, discussions, and arguments by noted scholars. Although the contents are usually the same in each book, no two copies are identical, varying in their elaborate decorations, notes, and inscribed interpretations. When passed down as family heirlooms, they often contain family trees and mementos of family ancestors from long past. (Cost: 15 koku. Rarity: 9.)

### New Armor

This section includes new armor options, particularly an option for protecting loyal equestrian steeds.

#### Mirror Armor

A specialized type of riding armor, mirror armor consists of four rounded metal discs that are buffed and polished to a mirror shine, then integrated into a tunic made of overlapping metal and leather scales. They are accompanied by a rounded helm. The mirror plates are believed to not only protect the wearer from arrows but also to blind malicious spirits, deflecting their attention.

#### Unicorn Barding

While most Rokugani war ponies are hindered in full barding, Unicorn barding utilizes several innovations to make armor lighter and more comfortable for the horse wearing it. Most barding is made from laminated plates set over a tent of padded cloth and includes a unique face covering similar to the war masks of samurai armor. Unicorn barding is generally considered superior, owing to better coverage, elaborate designs, and the care taken to ensure each piece of custom-made armor is comfortably fitted to the horse. Expensive and time-intensive to don, a suit of full Unicorn barding has become a status symbol for wealthy lords, worn more frequently in festivals and parades than for warfare. Unicorn barding can be worn only by horses, ponies, and other equine mounts.

The Talisman of the Sun is a meishōdō talisman that contains the following invocations from the core rulebook: By the Light of the Lord Moon (page 192), Hands of the Tides (page 208), and Wings of the Phoenix (page 206). After the talisman is used to cast an invocation, it cannot cast another until it has basked in sunlight for at least one minute. It does not have a stability value (see Stability, page 106)—for now, the spirit is trapped indefinitely.

## Blessed Treasures

The following blessed treasures are rare and unique items that are quite difficult to acquire. Most are nemuranai, or "awakened items" (see page 307 of the core rulebook). GMs may wish for characters to engage in long or extraordinary quests to locate or prove themselves worthy of such treasures.

### Treasures of the Unicorn

Presented here are but a few of the many treasures from the Unicorn's trove, reflecting their journeys across the lands and touchstones of their lore and glorious history.

#### Talisman of the Sun

During the Ki-Rin Clan's overthrow of the undead Sun Kings in Rempet alongside al-Qamari, a brave samurai heard a voice crying out to be freed from its prison. The source of these cries was a talisman of braided metal, bearing a jewel that shone like fire. The voice promised to aid its rescuers if only it could feel the sun again. It is said that after the Sun Kings' defeat, Iuchi made many attempts to free the imprisoned spirit from the talisman, as did his daughter and grandson after him, but to no avail. The Talisman of the Sun is now a treasure resting in Gatherer of Winds Castle, one of its many mysteries, brought out from its ornate container each morning by the seneschals to bask in the light of the sunrise.

#### Yuki: The Ancestral Armor of the Unicorn Clan

As the very armor that Shinjo wore when she left the Empire, Yuki would normally be considered one of the oldest examples of Rokugani armor. Yet over the centuries, the ancestral armor of the Unicorn Clan has seen many revisions and upgrades, even before it was finally gifted to Shinjo's son, Nishijin, before her confrontation with Orochi, the Great Serpent. On that day, Yuki's cream-white plates were painted midnight purple, a new regal color for a reborn clan. When the Unicorn returned, Yuki was unrecognizable with its white fur cloak, golden helmet-horns, and Qamari upgrades. But Shinjo's presence still lingers within, reminding the wearer of her promise to always return. While Yuki's base components are old-fashioned, many parts have been replaced over the years. The armor must be adjusted for each new champion, and each generation adds new improvements and embellishments. Thus, it has become a living embodiment of the Unicorn Clan's entire history, not merely its glorious past. It currently rests on display at Far Traveler Castle.

### Treasures of the Kitsune Forest

The following are a few of the treasures of the Kitsune Forest and touchstones of the Fox Clan.

#### The Ancestral Sword of the Fox Clan

As a part of the treaty that ended the Lion-Phoenix War and created the Fox Clan, the Emperor commissioned the forging of a grand sword to be presented

to the Fox Clan Champion. Agasha family blacksmiths forged a splendid blade, and after consultations with the daimyō of the Yogo family, the sword was inscribed with spiritual wards. Shortly after accepting the gift, the Fox Clan Champion disregarded it, setting it aside to languish—to him, the sword was simply a reminder of all the Fox had lost at the hands of the Great Clans, an attempt to placate the guilty consciences of those who had failed his people. Nearly three centuries later, a Fox samurai accidentally discovered that the blade had been replaced with a forgery. Yet few seemed to care; many assumed the forest denizens had taken it, and no one could even recall the sword's name. The ancestral sword of the Fox Clan has remained lost ever since—or at least, no one has yet admitted to finding it.

### Fox Pipe

The common superstition among Fox Clan samurai that one must never discuss secrets when one can smell smoke might be owed to the belief in "pipe foxes," ethereal spirits said to live within smoking pipes enchanted for this purpose. Such smoking pipes, which appear as ordinary kiseru that incorporate fox carvings on the bowl, are created as the result of a pact between a trickster spirit and a shugenja. Smoking the pipe causes an ethereal fox to rise from the bowl, ready to do the bidding of the smoker. This spirit is sent to spy on others unnoticed, reporting back to the smoker whatever it has heard. Folktales abound of lost fox pipes eventually making their way to market shops, where they are unknowingly purchased and the foxes within accidentally discovered. This is the origin of the Fox Clan colloquialism "To smoke up a fox," meaning to discover something useful in something believed to be useless. Awakened Item: Fox Pipes are nemuranai. As such, they have the Durable and Sacred item qualities. When a Fox Pipe casts its sealed invocation, the TN is decreased by 1, you may target a position at Range 0–4, and it lasts until you have finished smoking the pipe.

- Quirk: Smoking from this pipe causes a fox made of smoke to rise from the pipe, ready to spy for the smoker.
- Sealed Invocation: This pipe has Secrets on the Wind (core rulebook, page 194) sealed within it. The invocation may be activated once per game session as an action by smoking the pipe and using Courtesy (Air) instead of Theology (Air) on the check made to activate it.

