## Meishōdō Talismans

The core rulebook contains a simplified version of rules for meishōdō talismans in the Iuchi Meishōdō Master school ability, The Way of Names, on page 83. If a GM wishes to use more detailed rules for meishōdō talismans, they may use the following rules instead. If they do, the second paragraph of the Iuchi Meishōdō Master's school ability should be treated as reading: "You can sustain a number of meishōdō talismans up to your school rank. If you create one or more meishōdō talismans in excess of this limit, you must choose a number up to your school rank and then release the spirits contained within the rest." All other parts of the ability are replaced by the rules here.

### USING MEISHŌDŌ TALISMANS

A character carrying a meishōdō talisman can perform any invocations sealed within it. To do so, they make a check to activate the invocation using their skill ranks and the sealed spirit's ring value in place of their own. They still keep a total number of dice equal to their own ring, however.

### CREATING MEISHŌDŌ TALISMANS

A character who is a member of the Iuchi Meishōdō Master School or who has completed the Student of Names title (page 135) can create a meishōdō talisman by completing the Seal in an Object social objective (see page 128) during an otherworldly encounter (see page 125). This seals a spirit within the object. Once the talisman is made, the character determines the techniques it contains and its stability value.

### Techniques Contained

Meishōdō talismans are valuable because they contain techniques that can be accessed by any user, even one untrained in the Way of Names. To determine what technique is contained in a talisman, the creator of the talisman chooses one invocation matching the element of the highest ring of the sealed-in spirit. The rank of the invocation cannot exceed the value of the spirit's highest ring. If two or more rings are tied, the creator chooses one.

If the spirit is Tainted, the creator has the option of instead choosing one mahō technique in the same manner, with the same requirements.

Any techniques chosen this way are the techniques contained in the talisman.

### Stability

A meishōdō talisman also has a stability value. This value represents the likelihood that the spirit within will break free when the talisman is used. The stability value is determined at the time the talisman is created.

#### Determine Stability

To determine a talisman's stability value, add the creator's excess momentum points from the Seal in an Object social objective (see page 128) to their ranks in Design. Then, subtract the spirit's ranks in the Scholar skill group (or Theology, if it has individual skills). This value, to a minimum of 1, is the starting stability value. The higher the stability value, the harder it is for the spirit to break free.

If the stability of a talisman ever reaches 0, the spirit within shatters the talisman and is unleased on the world.

#### Losing Stability

Stability can be lowered in several ways:

## Item Patterns

Not all weapons are created equally. While two swords may appear identical, the artisans who forged them may have used different methods, different treatments, or different embellishments. Just as a work of art is a reflection of the artist, and as a story varies with the storyteller, so it is with weapons and their forgers.

There are two methods for gaining an item with one of the patterns described here. The first is for the character to apprentice themself to a master and learn the means of creating a new weapon with the desired pattern; this requires the character to win the master's trust within the game's story and then spend the required XP to learn the pattern. The other method is to requisition, purchase, or find an existing item with the pattern. The XP cost is the same, but the rarity is altered by the pattern's listed rarity modifier.

### Ujik Horsebow Pattern

The Ujik people of the western steppes boast a unique style of composite bow, one with a greater recurve and a long "bridge" to support the resting bowstring. When the drawn string is released, it strikes against the bridge with a loud crack, providing a boost in velocity and a greater punch. This sound, along with the tendency for Ujik-made arrows to whistle, has earned Ujik-style bows the nickname screeching bows among the other peoples along the Sand Road.

A bow made with the Ujik horsebow pattern gains +1 damage and the Snaring item quality due to its additional punching force. It loses the Durable item quality (and can never gain it), and if it is exposed to significant moisture, it gains the Damaged item quality.

### Utsuko Pattern

According to legend, when the Ki-Rin Clan divided itself into the five hordes to explore the lands beyond Rokugan, Iuchi asked Shinjo to forbid his eldest daughter, Meiko, from following him south with the Green Horde. Meiko begged Shinjo to send her south with her father, but instead, Meiko accompanied the Purple Horde with Utaku Shiko. During this time, she built upon the knowledge that her father had acquired, refining the art that would evolve into meishōdō. But it was her great-granddaughter, Iuchi Utsuko, who would complete her life's work, developing patterns for a more stable talisman. Artisans whose talismans utilize one of Iuchi Utsuko's patterns can create superior divine vessels for enshrined spirits to thrive within, or more perfect prisons for spirits that would otherwise terrorize the countryside.

After it is used to capture a spirit, a meishōdō talisman with the Utsuko pattern increases its stability by its creator's Void ring.

### Watered Steel Pattern

Named for the alternating patterns of light and dark bands inherent in the metal, which is said to resemble ripples in a watery surface, watered steel is a unique type of metal forged in the lands beyond the Sand Road. Watered steel is made primarily in the Caliphate via a secretive process that is protected by order of the caliph. Attempts to reproduce the steel have failed, leading many blacksmiths to believe there is more to this craft than mere pattern welding. Watered steel is prized in weaponry for its superior ability to hold an edge, its extreme tensile strength and flexibility, and its resistance to the elements.

An item made primarily of watered steel gains the Durable and Resplendent item qualities; if it is a weapon that already has the Durable item quality, it instead gains +1 deadliness. Additionally, Razor-Edged weapons made of watered steel gain the Hewing item quality (see page 116).

Armor cannot be made from watered steel.

## New Techniques

As with the techniques presented in the core rulebook, the new techniques here can be selected by a character who meets the prerequisites.

### NEW KATA

From the precise and deadly ax techniques of the Ganzu people to the skillful riding and archery of the Ujik, the following new techniques reflect the many unique fighting styles the Unicorn have encountered during their travels west.

#### General Kata

The following kata are flexible techniques with applications in ranged and melee combat alike.

#### Close-Combat Kata

The following kata are used for close-quarters combat.

#### Scouring Wind Style

As the wind in the desert carves the rocks over generations, so the warrior's weapon carves through their opponent's defenses. By hacking smoothly and repeatedly, the warrior's sustained onslaught eventually creates a gap through which they can deliver a fatal blow.

Activation: When you make an Attack action check using Martial Arts [Melee] or Martial Arts [Unarmed] while wielding a non-Razor-Edged weapon in two hands, you may spend ☯ as follows: ☯ ☯: If you succeed, instead of dealing damage, choose one defensive item your target is using (such as armor they are wearing or a readied shield they are wielding). The item receives the Damaged item quality; if it is already Damaged, it becomes Destroyed instead. ☯ ☯: If you succeed and you are wielding an ax, in addition to dealing damage, choose one defensive item your target is using (such as armor they are wearing or a readied shield they are wielding). It receives the Damaged item quality; if it is already Damaged, it becomes Destroyed instead.

#### Slashing Sandstorm Style

Thanks to the weight of its head, the length of its shaft, and its cutting edge, an ax generates incredible leverage in battle, potentially splitting skulls or severing limbs. In a whirl of blows, the warrior drives their foes before them, cutting down the weak and testing the resolve of the strong.

### Ranged Kata

The following kata are used primarily to engage a foe at range.

## NEW RITUALS

Humans create customs for everything, and for the Unicorn Clan, this includes customs for meeting with people with whom one shares few customs. The following are rituals that are especially useful for travelers, helping them find their way or make friends who can get them to their destination.

A number of characters up to your ranks in Culture can participate in a cultural exchange. Effects: If you succeed, each participant learns something of value about the culture of each other participant. Even if the participants do not speak the same language, they establish basic communication through gestures and a few learned words.

#### Protection of the Flock

Animals are key to the Ujik way of life: horses carry Ujik across the Plains of Wind and Stone; herd animals provide food, clothing, and shelter; and even predators in their environment, such as the massive eagles of the steppes, are often tamed for hunting.

#### Shadow of Days

The scholars of al-Zawira have unparalleled knowledge of the Mortal Realm's dimensions, the movement of stars in the sky, and the relationship between the two. Using mathematical models and knowledge of the world's scope, a scholar with this training can accurately calculate the distance between two locations, allowing them to estimate travel time, determine logistics, and more.

Effects: If you succeed, you determine the distance between the two locations as the crow flies in li, from which you can estimate travel days. The estimate does not necessarily account for all obstacles in the way.

> **New Opportunities**
>
> ❃: If you succeed and you have access to a map to cross-compare, you are able to create an even more accurate estimate of the travel time, accounting for known obstacles. ❃❃: Reduce the TN of any checks you make using the Recall approach by 1. This effect persists until the end of the next scene. ❃❃❃: You can accurately predict a future celestial phenomenon, such as the convergence of stellar bodies or an eclipse, in one or both locations.

#### Spiritual Survey

Iuchi spiritualists have spent centuries venturing into new lands and are skilled at quickly surveying a spiritual ecosystem they have entered. As the spiritual realms inevitably shape the natural world, the Iuchi have discovered that studying the flora and fauna of a land can teach one a great deal about the spirits that live there as well.

Activation: As a downtime activity, you may make a TN 3 Survival (Void) check to learn about local spirits and supernatural entities from the natural world around you. Effects: If you succeed, you learn the nature of the spirits that inhabit the region of the world around you (this effect extends 6 range bands if a specific range is needed). You learn their elemental affinities, any otherworldly realms from which they hail, their preferred offerings, and their general inclinations toward humans. These spirits become aware that someone extended their senses toward them, but not your specific location within the area.

#### Traveler's Experience

Many travelers learn the fine art of quickly acclimating themselves to a new place: speaking to the locals, trying regional cuisines, and observing the local customs and laws. By observing the inhabitants of a place of human habitation for a short time, a veteran traveler can take in the location's feel so they can operate effectively there.

Activation: As a downtime activity in a place of habitation where you are unfamiliar with the laws and customs (of humans or another sentient species), you may make a TN 2 Culture (Earth) check to observe and determine the customs and protocols of this new place you have reached.

Effects: If you succeed, you quickly ascertain the laws and cultural norms of the place you are currently visiting. You are able to logically discern whether a particular proposed course of action is legal or illegal and whether it is rude or polite.

### New Opportunities

- 🌀🌀: Reduce the TN of any checks you make using the Recall approach by 1. This effect persists until the end of the next scene.
- 🌀🌀: Through study and use of common roots in languages you do speak, you are able to make yourself understood in the common language of the place you are visiting. While you are not fluent in the language, you are intelligible.

#### Wayfinder's Instincts

Survival in a marginal environment means making oneself aware of the subtlest details in the area. A desert guide on the Sand Road or an Ujik nomad in the steppe can find minute traces of water, tracks upon shifting sand, or signs of a nearby outcropping that can provide shelter from the cutting winds.

Effects: If you succeed, choose one of the following key resources: potable water, food, or shelter. You come upon a sign of the nearest source of that resource, and you are able to determine the direction you must travel to reach it.

### NEW SHŪJI

Whether one is in a Rokugani court, within the heart of the Qamarist Caliphate, or before the ordu of a powerful khan in the Plains of Wind and Stone, some truths about places where the powerful gather remain the same. In the presence of great lords, one can always find courtiers of a sort, individuals skilled in pursuing their own gains along with those of their political backers. The following shūji are useful in any courtly environment, wherever it may be found and whatever form it takes.

### AIR SHŪJI

Air shūji are the rhetoric of subtlety and sensitivity: perceiving the fine details of the world, others, and oneself, and then using them to the greatest effect possible.

#### Cunning Omission

It is said by some courtiers that the easiest way to get someone to admit something is not through blackmail or coercion, but simply by explaining the situation to them incorrectly and letting their impulse to set the record straight fill in the details. By leaving out a key element in a story or account, a courtier attempts to draw their opponent into providing it themselves, thus undermining their own argument or offering up the testimony needed to prove the initial courtier's point.

Effects: If you succeed, your target slips up and admits something they were concealing from you. If your target was not concealing anything from you, you now know this fact with certainty.

#### Entice with Offerings

Negotiation is always a matter of give and take, and sometimes the key to taking what you want is giving up something your adversary values more highly than you do. In fact, a careful concession can even turn an enemy into an unwitting ally by setting up bait that, when swallowed, makes the foe's efforts serve one's own agenda.

☯ ☯: Instead of being unable to add momentum to their social objectives, when your target would otherwise add one or more momentum points to one of their social objectives, those momentum points are added to one of your social objectives of your choice instead. Your target is not aware that their efforts are being diverted to your cause.

#### Trader's Intuition

Most Rokugani samurai do not often deal with commerce personally, instead relying on servants to buy and sell goods for them if needed. In the Unicorn Clan, the line between merchants and warrior nobility is not so clearly demarcated. Many powerful Unicorn deal with their own finances, not just balancing the books but actually trading goods at the markets of Khanbulak and in cities far beyond Rokugan.

☯: You know a place (which you have visited) where you could sell the object for more money than it is worth in your current location.

### Earth Shūji

Earth shūji often focus on the material: what is known for certain, can be shown demonstrably, or is evidenced by actions rather than mere words.

#### Cite the Facts

Some arguments' winners are determined by rhetoric, but others come down to hard facts. Of course, bludgeoning one's opponent with more data than they can respond to is itself a rhetorical tactic, and one that can be quite effective against someone less well-prepared.

Activation: As a Scheme action check, you may make a Scholar skill (Earth) check targeting another character using a Scholar skill (or other skill, at your GM's discretion) relevant to the topic at hand. The TN of this check is equal to your target's focus minus their current strife, to a minimum of 1. Effects: If you succeed, your target suffers the Silenced condition unless they choose to gain strife equal to your ranks in Command, plus one for every bonus success.

- ☘: If you succeed, add one bonus success to this check for each reserved die you have.
- ☘☘+: Choose one additional target with a focus lower than the original target's per ☘☘ spent this way.

#### Solidify Gratitude

Gratitude can be a powerful shield or an insidious weapon, especially when a courtier is alone in a faraway court. For members of the Ide Emissary School (see page 84), being sent to courts across the world where one must act without support or long-standing relationships is the norm. As such, the Ide tradition teaches its students to make friends quickly—especially with one's enemies.

- ☘☘: You learn your target's reason for pursuing their social objective if you do not already know it.
- ☘☘: You gain your target's assistance on the next check you make. This effect persists until the end of the scene.

### Fire Shūji

Fire shūji often focus on controlling surges of emotion, drawing out the extremes in people or winning their awe through dramatic statements and gestures.

#### Glorious Entrance

A courtier who often travels from place to place knows the importance of making an entrance. After all, a first impression can set the tone for all future negotiations. Whether arriving in style or simply making a bold proclamation to attract attention, a courtier who knows how to draw all eyes to themself sets the table for all forthcoming negotiations.

Activation: When you enter a scene, you may make a Performance (Fire) check. The TN of this check is equal to the difference between the glory rank of the character in the scene with the highest glory and your glory rank, to a minimum of 1. Effects: If you succeed, reduce the TN of Scheme and Support action checks you make by 1. This effect persists for one round, plus one additional round for every two bonus successes.

- ☘☘+: When you receive a glory award, increase the amount of glory you gain by 1, plus 1 per additional ☘☘ spent this way.
- ☘☘: You gain the Emboldened condition (see page 133).

#### Incite Wrath

Whether a war mount is a horse, camel, or elephant, it is not just a way for a warrior to move more quickly on the battlefield but also a living weapon unto itself. A skillful rider can drive their mount to lash out with hooves, tusks, or teeth, smashing foes aside with its might.

Activation: When you make a Scheme or Support action check while riding a mount or while you have an animal companion at range 0–1 of you, you may spend ☘ as follows:

☸ ☸+: Your mount or animal companion immediately performs a Strike action targeting one character within range of one of its readied weapons. The damage it deals increases by 1 per ☸ ☸ spent this way.

### Water Shūji

Water shūji rest on the strength of compromise, collaborative effort, and synthesizing disparate ideas into a useful whole.

#### Call to Ride

A skilled rider can train their loyal steed to come to them on command. When the rider takes a long journey through perilous lands, this technique can be especially vital for escaping the chaos of battle or pursuing marauders who have struck at their caravan.

Activation: As a Support action, you can make a TN 1 Survival (Water) check targeting your mount or animal companion, provided it is within range 5 of you. Effects: If you succeed, your mount or animal companion immediately moves up to 2 range bands toward you, plus 1 additional range band for every two bonus successes. If it is a mount and ends at range 0 of you, you can immediately mount it.

> **New Opportunities**
>
> ☸ ☸: Increase the TN of Attack action checks targeting you and your mount or animal companion by 1. This effect persists until the end of your next turn. ☸ ☸: If you perform a Maneuver action after mounting, you may move up to 2 range bands instead of 1.

#### Complementary Experience

Two heads are better than one, or so the saying goes, in part because those two heads do not have identical experiences. By working with someone with a markedly different skill set, a courtier can expand their tactics significantly and find solutions they might never have considered seeking.

#### Everyone Has a Price

Some Rokugani samurai would balk at the thought of paying off bandits or marauders rather than bringing them to justice, but when traveling the Sand Road, pragmatism often wins out. After all, many parts of the Sand Road pass through other lands, with their own laws and customs, and becoming involved in a feud over a few coins hardly seems worthwhile. Ide Emissaries and others who travel outside of Rokugan know the value of making a mutually beneficial deal, even with one's enemies.

Activation: As a Scheme action, you may make a Sentiment (Water) check targeting another character in the scene. The TN of this check is equal to the target's honor rank. You must offer a bribe with a value in koku equal to the target's 💰 threat rating to attempt this check. At the GM's discretion, this does not work on mindless or inscrutable Otherworldly creatures, such as undead. Effects: If you succeed and your target is a Minion NPC, they accept the bribe and leave the scene. If you succeed and your target is not a Minion NPC, they must accept the bribe unless they receive strife equal to your ranks in Courtesy. If they accept the bribe, they must either leave the scene, stop attempting to oppose you, or seek terms with you in some sort of negotiation. At the GM's discretion, this might deescalate a more serious conflict to an intrigue.

#### Horse Whisperer

The unity of horse and rider is the ideal state between the samurai and their steed, relying on the mutual trust and genuine respect each has developed for the other. The rider who exemplifies this knows that their horse is not a mere mode of transportation, but a comrade-in-arms, a friendship that ultimately culminates in one becoming an extension of the other.

> **BATTLE MAIDENS AND HORSE WHISPERER**
>
> At the GM's discretion, Utaku Battle Maiden PCs may replace Touchstone of Courage with Horse Whisperer in their school curriculum.

- 🌸 🌸: Give the target the Loyal Steed ability—it can be ridden by a human-sized (silhouette 2 or lower) rider—until the end of the scene.

### Void Shūji

Void shūji are frequently focused on perspective and on helping oneself or others to see things in a useful way.

> **Appreciate the Scenery**
>
> Rank 1

In many lands, including Rokugan, the beauty of the natural world inspires art, poetry, and other works of note, even (or perhaps especially) when it is stark and dangerous. When on a long and perilous journey into foreign lands where deadly intrigues surely await, finding beauty in the rougher parts of the world can be a much-needed source of solace.

Activation: When you make a Support action check targeting another character, if you and the character you are targeting are in Dangerous, Entangling, Hallowed, Imbalanced, or Obscuring terrain, you may spend 🌸 as follows: Earth or Water 🌸: You and your target each remove strife equal to your ranks in Composition. Air or Fire 🌸: You and your target gain the Emboldened condition (see page 133). Void 🌸: Your and your target gain the Centered condition (see page 133).

### New Water Invocation

A single new water invocation to enhance the abilities of steeds.

> **The World is Not Heavy**
>
> Rank 2

Benevolence of the Tidepool (Elemental), Carried by Herd-Back (Foreign), Essence of the Floating World (Alchemy)

When the illusions of the material world are lifted, even temporarily, nothing seems particularly heavy anymore.

Activation: As a Support action, you may make a TN 3 Theology (Water) check targeting yourself or a living creature you are touching with your bare hands. Effects: If you succeed, you augment the target's body with the buoyancy of the water kami. The target gains the ability to carry items with a maximum combined silhouette value equal to the target's own silhouette plus 1. Additionally, their armor loses the Cumbersome item quality while they are wearing it. If the target is a steed, the maximum combined silhouette value of riders it can comfortably carry increases by 2—but none of these riders may individually have a silhouette value greater than the steed can normally carry. These effects persist for the duration of the scene.

## New Item Quality

The new item quality listed here—Hewing—may be considered part of the item quality list on page 240 of the Legend of the Five Rings roleplaying game core rulebook.

### Hewing

This weapon is specifically designed to bypass or break through shields and armor. When you make an Attack action check with a Hewing weapon, you may spend 🌸 as follows: 🌸: This attack ignores any TN bonuses from an opponent's shield. 🌸+: Decrease the target's physical resistance by 1 for each 🌸 spent this way.
